# Every documentation citation that does not resolve, and why it is allowed to stay.
#
# `.github/scripts/citations.sh check` derives the citation set from the tree
# (`.github/scripts/citations`) and compares it against this file in both
# directions: unresolved in the tree but missing here is red, and listed here
# while the citation resolves again -- or while no document makes it any more --
# is red too. So a fabricated symbol cannot land quietly, and a line here cannot
# outlive the citation it waives.
#
# Nothing declares which documents are covered or which backticked words are
# citations. Both come out of the tree, and this file lists only the waivers, so
# a new document joins the lane by existing. A register OF citations would fail
# open at exactly the moment it has to fail closed.
#
# docs/plans/ is NOT covered, and that is a decision about the document class
# rather than about the size of this file. A plan is dated: its citations were
# true on the day it was written, nobody maintains it afterwards, and the
# correct state of a June plan citing a since-renamed symbol is unchanged. A
# ratchet over documents that are never supposed to change is a ratchet that
# cannot reach zero. The reasoning is at docFiles in
# .github/scripts/citations/main.go. ADRs stay covered: CLAUDE.md cites 0017 as
# governing rules soldiers work under today, so a stale citation in one is a
# real defect.
#
# This is a ratchet. It is only supposed to get shorter. The reason column is
# self-declared, so a waiver nobody would accept keeps this green -- reading the
# diff is what catches that, not the script.
#
# What a reason owes is governed by ADR-0017 §4 (premise, invalidation condition,
# pinning test). Every row is argued individually with a premise (a) and an
# invalidation condition (b). For part (c), where the premise is that the cited
# symbol or file is gone or belongs to another tree/runtime layout, this lane's
# two-way comparison serves the pinning function (reintroducing the symbol or
# file resolves the citation and causes the `removed` check to fail). Where the
# premise is about what a document says, it is unpinned, sharing the same stated
# limit as .github/deadcode.allow.
#
# Columns are tab-separated: document <TAB> kind <TAB> citation <TAB> reason
#
# No line numbers, in the key or in the citation: `internal/x/y.go:41` is keyed
# and checked as `internal/x/y.go`. Run `.github/scripts/citations.sh list` for
# the current keys, and `.github/scripts/citations.sh unchecked` for the
# citations no rule here judges at all -- those take no line in this file, and
# `check` prints their count on every run so this file is not mistaken for a
# complete account of what the documents claim.
#
# A reason starting with OPEN-BUG marks a citation known to be wrong rather than
# accepted debt; `check` reprints every one as a warning on every run (ADR-0017
# §5). There are none today.

# --- Documents whose citation is deliberate: the absence is the claim ----------
# These do not resolve and are not defects. Each names something the document itself says is gone,
# belongs to another tree or to a munsu home rather than to this repo, or is not a Go declaration at
# all. They are the shape of citation this lane cannot distinguish from a stale one, and each line
# says why a reader should not go looking. 81 lines.
